$b['A cluster of blackberries covered in cold morning condensation. Individual berries shift from deep purple-black to ruby and blue where window light passes across their wet surfaces'] = 'Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A cluster of blackberries covered in cold morning condensation. Individual berries shift from deep purple-black to ruby and blue where window light passes across their wet surfaces. Lush, tactile, jewel-like. In the right panel it is on a woman, outer upper arm. COLOUR REALISM RULE REALISM MAKES IT BELIEVABLE. COLOR MAKES IT ALIVE. LIGHT MAKES THE COLOR MAKE SENSE. DO NOT COLOR A REALISM TATTOO. BUILD REALITY OUT OF COLOR. REALISM REQUIREMENTS Everything must obey believable: Anatomy Perspective Material behavior Light direction Reflection Refraction Transparency Surface texture Atmospheric depth Color contamination Shadow Depth of field where appropriate Do not create plastic-looking skin. Do not create generic AI gloss. Do not outline realistic objects unless the actual subject produces that edge. Do not turn every color to maximum saturation. Use natural muted color beside intense color so the intense areas have power. Black may contain reflected color. White may contain environmental color. Skin contains warm and cool variation. Water changes the colors beneath it. Glass bends the image behind it. Metal reflects its environment. Wet surfaces behave differently from dry surfaces. Translucent material allows light through it. Fire illuminates nearby objects. Neon contaminates nearby color. THE COLOR MUST BELONG TO THE WORLD. ARTISTIC RANGE Some pieces can be small hyperreal studies. Some can be portraits. Some can be animals. Some can be botanical. Some can be objects. Some can be landscapes. Some can be full cinematic scenes. Some can be surreal. Some can become large statement tattoos. Do not make all 100 isolated objects. Do not make all 100 portraits. Do not make all 100 bright. Do not make all 100 pretty. Allow: Darkness Rain Fire Night Cold Heat Luxury Nature Predators Human emotion Ocean City Storm Surrealism Intimacy Scale When an impossible subject is used: DO NOT RENDER IT LIKE FANTASY ART. Render the impossible event as though a camera could actually photograph it. That distinction is essential. BODY DESIGN Design every tattoo specifically for the stated body placement. Use anatomy as part of the composition. A portrait may wrap subtly around the upper arm. A wave may follow the thigh. A bird may use the shoulder for flight. A long reflective object may follow the forearm. A large animal may move around shoulder musculature. A cinematic landscape may use the thigh as a larger vertical canvas. Do not simply create rectangular photographs and paste them onto skin. The artwork must remain realism while becoming tattoo composition. MANDATORY TWO-PANEL PRESENTATION EVERY SINGLE IMAGE MUST CONTAIN TWO PANELS. THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL. LEFT PANEL, WHITE ART CARD Show the COMPLETE tattoo artwork by itself on a clean bright white background. No skin. No human body. No mockup. No photograph of a tattoo. No decorative environment outside the tattoo composition. This is the clean standalone tattoo artwork. The entire design must be visible. Leave comfortable white space around it. The white panel must be immediately obvious when viewing the entire category sheet. RIGHT PANEL, BODY Show the EXACT SAME artwork tattooed realistically on the specified human body placement. The body panel is NOT a second interpretation. DO NOT redraw the subject. DO NOT change the lighting inside the artwork. DO NOT change colors. DO NOT change composition. DO NOT add or remove elements. DO NOT flip the artwork unless physical orientation explicitly requires it. The viewer must instantly understand: LEFT = THE ARTWORK RIGHT = THAT EXACT ARTWORK AS A TATTOO BODY REALISM On the right panel: Preserve real skin texture. Allow pores. Allow natural skin variation. Allow body curvature. Allow believable photographic lighting. The tattoo must conform naturally to anatomy. It must appear embedded in skin rather than printed on top of it. Do not blur the tattoo excessively. Do not change the design because it is being shown on skin. Show enough body to make the placement clear. Do not let the human model become the subject of the card. THE TATTOO IS THE SUBJECT. FINAL STANDARD LEFT IS CLEAN ART ON WHITE. RIGHT IS THE EXACT SAME ART ON THE BODY. REALISM MAKES IT BELIEVABLE. COLOR MAKES IT ALIVE. LIGHT MAKES THE COLOR MAKE SENSE. MAKE PEOPLE LOOK AT THE TATTOO AND WONDER HOW THAT COLOR, LIGHT, WATER, GLASS, FUR, FIRE OR SKIN COULD POSSIBLY BE INK'
