al studies. Some can be portraits. Some can be animals. Some can be botanical. Some can be objects. Some can be landscapes. Some can be full cinematic scenes. Some can be surreal. Some can become large statement tattoos. Do not make all 100 isolated objects. Do not make all 100 portraits. Do not make all 100 bright. Do not make all 100 pretty. Allow: Darkness Rain Fire Night Cold Heat Luxury Nature Predators Human emotion Ocean City Storm Surrealism Intimacy Scale When an impossible subject is used: DO NOT RENDER IT LIKE FANTASY ART. Render the impossible event as though a camera could actually photograph it. That distinction is essential. BODY DESIGN Design every tattoo specifically for the stated body placement. Use anatomy as part of the composition. A portrait may wrap subtly around the upper arm. A wave may follow the thigh. A bird may use the shoulder for flight. A long reflective object may follow the forearm. A large animal may move around shoulder musculature. A cinematic landscape may use the thigh as a larger vertical canvas. Do not simply create rectangular photographs and paste them onto skin. The artwork must remain realism while becoming tattoo composition. MANDATORY TWO-PANEL PRESENTATION EVERY SINGLE IMAGE MUST CONTAIN TWO PANELS. THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL. LEFT PANEL, WHITE ART CARD Show the COMPLETE tattoo artwork by itself on a clean bright white background. No skin. No human body. No mockup. No photograph of a tattoo. No decorative environment outside the tattoo composition. This is the clean standalone tattoo artwork. The entire design must be visible. Leave comfortable white space around it. The white panel must be immediately obvious when viewing the entire category sheet. RIGHT PANEL, BODY Show the EXACT SAME artwork tattooed realistically on the specified human body placement. The body panel is NOT a second interpretation. DO NOT redraw the subject. DO NOT change the lighting inside the artwork. DO NOT change colors. DO NOT change composition. D